Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF)

"If a browser is logged in, the browser will obey."
CSRF abuses the browser's automatic trust in authenticated sessions.
Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF) is a web vulnerability where an attacker tricks a victim’s browser into sending a request to a web application where the victim is already authenticated.
Because the browser automatically includes authentication cookies, the server believes the request is legitimate.
The attacker does not need to steal credentials — they simply abuse the victim's existing authenticated session.


1. Core Idea of CSRF
The fundamental idea behind CSRF is simple:

Server trusts browser
Browser trusts user
Attacker abuses that trust
When a user logs into a website:

User logs in
       │
       ▼
Server creates session
       │
       ▼
Browser stores session cookie
       │
       ▼
Browser automatically attaches cookie to every request
This automatic cookie attachment is the root cause of CSRF.
The browser cannot distinguish between:

Request made intentionally by user
vs
Request triggered by malicious website


2. Why CSRF Happens
CSRF exists because of three conditions:
1️⃣ Authentication via cookies
Most web applications use session cookies for authentication.
Example:

Cookie: sessionid=abc123
The browser automatically attaches this cookie to every request sent to the domain.


2️⃣ Browser automatically sends cookies
Example request:

POST /transfer-money
Cookie: sessionid=abc123
Even if the request is triggered by a malicious website, the browser still attaches the cookie.


3️⃣ Server does not verify request origin
If the server only checks authentication cookie, the request will succeed.
This means:

Authenticated cookie present → request trusted
Which leads to CSRF.


3. High Level Attack Flow

Victim logs into bank.com
        │
        ▼
Browser stores session cookie
        │
        ▼
Victim visits attacker.com
        │
        ▼
Attacker page triggers hidden request
        │
        ▼
Browser automatically attaches bank.com cookie
        │
        ▼
Bank processes request as authenticated user
The attacker never sees the response.
They only need the action to succeed.


4. Simple CSRF Example
Imagine a banking application.
Money transfer endpoint:

POST /transfer
Request body:

amount=5000
to_account=attacker_account


Legitimate request
User intentionally transfers money.

POST /transfer
Cookie: sessionid=abc123

amount=5000
to_account=friend


CSRF attack
Attacker creates a malicious webpage:

<form action="https://bank.com/transfer" method="POST">
  <input type="hidden" name="amount" value="5000">
  <input type="hidden" name="to_account" value="attacker">
</form>

<script>
document.forms[0].submit();
</script>
If the victim visits this page while logged in:

Browser sends request automatically
Result:

POST /transfer
Cookie: sessionid=abc123

amount=5000
to_account=attacker
The bank processes the transfer.


