8. Why GET Requests Are Dangerous
Some developers mistakenly allow state changes via GET requests.
Example:

GET /delete-account?id=123
This is extremely dangerous.
An attacker can trigger this with:

<img src="https://site.com/delete-account?id=123">
When the page loads:

Browser automatically sends the request
This makes exploitation trivial.


Key Takeaway
CSRF works because:

Browser automatically sends authentication cookies
AND
Server does not verify request legitimacy
This allows attackers to execute actions on behalf of authenticated users.

9. Types of CSRF Attacks
CSRF attacks are not limited to simple form submissions.
Modern attackers exploit multiple variations depending on how the application behaves.
The most common CSRF variants include:

Classic CSRF
GET-based CSRF
JSON CSRF
Login CSRF
Multi-step CSRF
Stored CSRF
Understanding these variants is important because many modern applications mistakenly believe they are protected while still being vulnerable to advanced forms.

10. Classic Form-Based CSRF
The most common CSRF attack uses a hidden form.
Example:

<form action="https://bank.com/transfer" method="POST">
  <input type="hidden" name="amount" value="10000">
  <input type="hidden" name="to_account" value="attacker">
</form>

<script>
document.forms[0].submit();
</script>
Attack flow:

Victim logged into bank.com
        │
        ▼
Victim opens attacker page
        │
        ▼
Hidden form auto-submits
        │
        ▼
Browser sends session cookie
        │
        ▼
Bank processes transfer
This is the traditional CSRF scenario.

11. GET-Based CSRF
Some applications mistakenly allow state-changing operations through GET requests.
Example vulnerable endpoint:

GET /change-email?email=attacker@mail.com
An attacker can exploit this using:

<img src="https://site.com/change-email?email=attacker@mail.com">
or

<a href="https://site.com/delete-account?id=123">
Click here for free gift
</a>
When the victim loads the page:

Browser automatically sends the request
Because cookies are attached automatically, the server treats it as a valid authenticated request.

12. JSON CSRF
Many developers assume that JSON APIs are safe from CSRF.
This assumption is incorrect.
Example API endpoint:

POST /api/change-email
Content-Type: application/json
Request body:

{
 "email":"attacker@mail.com"
}
Developers often believe:

"Attackers cannot send JSON requests from HTML forms"
But attackers can bypass this using content-type confusion or other request tricks.
Example attack:

<form action="https://site.com/api/change-email" method="POST">
<input name='{"email":"attacker@mail.com"}'>
</form>
If the backend parser is weak, it may interpret this as valid JSON.
Another attack vector is CORS misconfiguration, where malicious JavaScript can send cross-origin API requests.

13. Login CSRF
Login CSRF is a less obvious but dangerous variant.
Instead of performing actions as the victim, the attacker forces the victim to log into an attacker-controlled account.
Attack scenario:

Attacker creates account on site
        │
        ▼
Attacker generates login CSRF page
        │
        ▼
Victim visits malicious page
        │
        ▼
Browser logs victim into attacker account
Result:

Victim unknowingly uses attacker account
Impact:

Victim uploads personal data
Victim enters payment info
Victim performs actions tied to attacker account
The attacker can later access this data from their own account.

14. Multi-Step CSRF
Some applications implement workflows such as:

Step 1 → Add beneficiary
Step 2 → Confirm transfer
Step 3 → Execute payment
Developers assume this prevents CSRF.
However attackers can still automate the entire workflow.
Example attack sequence:

Request 1 → Add attacker account as beneficiary
Request 2 → Initiate transfer
Request 3 → Confirm payment
All requests are triggered automatically when the victim loads the malicious page.
This is known as multi-step CSRF exploitation.

15. Stored CSRF
Stored CSRF happens when the malicious request is stored on the target application itself.
Example scenario:

Forum post
User profile field
Stored message
Comment section
An attacker embeds a malicious request inside content.
Example:

<img src="https://bank.com/transfer?amount=5000&to=attacker">
When other users view the page:

Browser automatically sends the request
This turns a single CSRF attack into a mass exploitation scenario.

16. Why CSRF Works Even Without JavaScript
Many people think CSRF requires JavaScript.
This is not true.
CSRF can be triggered using simple HTML elements:

<form>
<img>
<iframe>
<link>
<script>
Example:

<img src="https://site.com/delete-account">
The browser automatically requests the resource.
This is why CSRF defenses must be implemented server-side, not client-side.

17. CSRF vs XSS
These two vulnerabilities are often confused.
Key difference:

CSRF → forces victim to perform actions
XSS → allows attacker to run JavaScript
Comparison:

CSRF
Attacker cannot read response
Victim must be authenticated
Relies on browser cookie behavior

XSS
Attacker executes JavaScript
Can steal cookies and tokens
Can perform CSRF-like actions
Important note:

XSS can bypass CSRF protections
Because JavaScript running inside the application can access CSRF tokens.

18. Modern Browser Protections
Modern browsers introduced protections like:

SameSite cookies
CORS policies
Origin header checks
However these protections reduce risk but do not eliminate CSRF completely.
Applications must still implement proper server-side defenses.

Key Takeaway
CSRF is not just a simple form attack.
It includes multiple advanced variants:

Form CSRF
GET CSRF
JSON CSRF
Login CSRF
Multi-step CSRF
Stored CSRF
Understanding these patterns helps security engineers identify vulnerabilities that developers often overlook.

19. Detecting CSRF Vulnerabilities
CSRF vulnerabilities usually appear when state-changing requests do not include any anti-forgery validation.
Security engineers test CSRF using the following methodology.
Step 1 — Identify Sensitive Actions
Look for endpoints that perform state-changing operations:

Transfer money
Change email
Change password
Delete account
Add payment method
Modify permissions
These endpoints are high-value CSRF targets.


Step 2 — Check if the Request Requires a Token
Example request:

POST /change-email
Cookie: session=abc123

email=new@mail.com
If the request does not include a CSRF token, it may be vulnerable.
A secure request usually looks like:

POST /change-email
Cookie: session=abc123

email=new@mail.com
csrf_token=8f93hs82hs


Step 3 — Try Replaying the Request Without the Token
Using a proxy like Burp Suite:
	1. Capture a request
	2. Remove the CSRF token
	3. Replay the request
If the server still processes the request, the endpoint is vulnerable.


Step 4 — Try Executing the Request From Another Origin
Create a simple attack page.
Example:

<form action="https://site.com/change-email" method="POST">
<input type="hidden" name="email" value="attacker@mail.com">
</form>

<script>
document.forms[0].submit();
</script>
If the request succeeds when the victim visits the page, the application is vulnerable.


20. CSRF Protection Mechanisms
There are multiple defenses against CSRF.
However, not all of them are equally strong.
The strongest protection is CSRF tokens.


21. CSRF Tokens (Anti-Forgery Tokens)
A CSRF token is a random secret value generated by the server and tied to the user session.
Example secure request:

POST /transfer
Cookie: session=abc123

amount=100
to_account=456
csrf_token=91f8hs82hsh
Why This Works
Attackers cannot generate valid tokens because:

Token is generated by the server
Token is tied to the user session
Token is unpredictable
Therefore the malicious site cannot forge the request correctly.


Token Validation Flow

User loads form
        │
        ▼
Server generates CSRF token
        │
        ▼
Token embedded in form
        │
        ▼
User submits request
        │
        ▼
Server validates token
        │
        ▼
Request accepted or rejected


Example Implementation
Server generates token:

csrf_token = random_secure_value()
store_in_session(csrf_token)
Form includes token:

<input type="hidden" name="csrf_token" value="91f8hs82hsh">
Server validates:

if request.csrf_token != session.csrf_token
    reject request


22. Synchronizer Token Pattern
This is the most secure CSRF defense.
Architecture:

Token stored in session
Token sent inside form
Server validates token
Diagram:

Server
 │
 │ generates CSRF token
 ▼
Session Storage
 │
 ▼
HTML Form

<form>
<input type="hidden" name="csrf_token">
</form>
When the form is submitted:

Request Token == Session Token
If not:

Request rejected


23. Double Submit Cookie Pattern
Another approach is double submit cookies.
Here the token is stored in both cookie and request body.
Example:

Cookie: csrf_token=abc123
Request:

POST /update

csrf_token=abc123
Server checks:

Cookie Token == Request Token
If both match, the request is accepted.
However, this approach is slightly weaker than synchronizer tokens.


24. SameSite Cookies
Modern browsers introduced the SameSite cookie attribute to reduce CSRF risk.
Example cookie:

Set-Cookie: session=abc123; SameSite=Lax
SameSite options:

Strict
Lax
None
Behavior:

Strict → cookie never sent cross-site
Lax → cookie sent only on safe navigation
None → cookie sent everywhere
Example protection:

Set-Cookie: session=abc123; SameSite=Strict
This prevents the browser from sending cookies during cross-site requests.


25. Origin and Referer Validation
Servers can also check the Origin or Referer header.
Example request:

Origin: https://site.com
If the request comes from:

Origin: https://attacker.com
The server rejects it.
Example validation logic:

if request.origin != expected_origin
    reject request
However this method has limitations:

Some browsers remove referer
Privacy extensions block headers
Some corporate proxies modify them
Therefore Origin validation should be used as an additional layer, not the primary defense.


26. Why CAPTCHA Is Not CSRF Protection
Many applications attempt to prevent CSRF using CAPTCHA.
This is not a reliable defense.
Reasons:

CAPTCHA only blocks automated bots
CSRF uses real users
CAPTCHA can sometimes be bypassed
Therefore CAPTCHA should never be the primary CSRF defense.


27. Production Secure Architecture Checklist
Security engineers should verify these controls.
Authentication Layer

Session cookies use SameSite
Cookies marked Secure
Cookies marked HttpOnly
Application Layer

CSRF tokens implemented
Tokens validated server-side
Tokens tied to session
Tokens rotated periodically
API Layer

State-changing APIs require tokens
CORS properly restricted
No wildcard origins
Request Validation

Origin validation implemented
Referer validation used where possible


28. Secure Coding Patterns
Java Example
Token generation:

String csrfToken = UUID.randomUUID().toString();
session.setAttribute("csrf_token", csrfToken);
Validation:

String requestToken = request.getParameter("csrf_token");
String sessionToken = session.getAttribute("csrf_token");

if(!requestToken.equals(sessionToken)){
    throw new SecurityException("Invalid CSRF token");
}


Python Example
Token generation:

import secrets

csrf_token = secrets.token_hex(32)
session["csrf_token"] = csrf_token
Validation:

if request.form["csrf_token"] != session["csrf_token"]:
    raise Exception("Invalid CSRF token")


