Why It Happens
Because browsers automatically attach authentication cookies to requests.


Attack Scenario

Victim logged into bank
Victim visits malicious page
Malicious page sends request to bank
Browser attaches cookies
Bank executes request


Impact

Fund transfers
Account modification
Privilege escalation
Data manipulation


Mitigation

CSRF tokens
SameSite cookies
Origin validation
Secure API design


Key Takeaway
CSRF exists because browsers automatically trust cookies.
Therefore protection requires server-side verification that the request truly came from the legitimate application.
The most reliable defenses are:

CSRF Tokens
SameSite Cookies
Origin Validation
Secure API Architecture
